        "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e83{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e83{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e83{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int (3{t}^{2}-5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{3}-5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|t|+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|t|\u27e9+\u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|t|\u27e9+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
